给他一辆特别的货车，因为他收到了太多的支持信。成千上万封信。最著名的是，数百名来自东区的码头工人游行前往威斯敏斯特，举着标语，写着“别抨击伊诺克”，或者“支持英国，而不是黑人英国”。他们呼吁希思让他回到影子内阁，担任影子国防发言人。你能想象英国历史上还有哪个时刻，码头工人会关心谁在影子内阁里吗？他们甚至不是真正的内阁成员。

110
00:56:36.000 --> 00:57:07.000
I mean, it's mind-boggling. And we can talk in a few minutes about where this leads politically. So Powell himself, this makes him, of course, a pariah to liberals, but this really does make him the tribune of the plebs to people who agree with him. Polls again and again in the next few years find that he's one of the most admired politicians, if not the most admired politician in the country. There's a brilliant book, actually, of people who are interested in kind of grassroots opinion by a journalist called Jeremy Seabrook, called City Close Up,
我的意思是，这太令人难以置信了。我们几分钟后可以谈谈这在政治上会导致什么。所以鲍威尔本人，这当然使他成为自由派眼中的贱民，但这确实使他成为那些同意他观点的人心目中的平民代言人。在接下来的几年里，民意调查一次又一次地显示，他是该国最受钦佩的政治家之一，如果不是最受钦佩的话。事实上，有一本关于那些对基层舆论感兴趣的人的精彩书籍，是由一位名叫杰里米·西布鲁克的记者写的，书名叫《城市特写》，

111
00:57:07.000 --> 00:57:29.000
and he went to Blackburn at the end of the 60s, early 70s, and interviewed loads of people, hung around working men's clubs and stuff. And these are people who don't follow Westminster politics. You know, very, very sort of small, oh, ordinary people. And Powell's name came up again and again. He's the finest man in the country. He should be prime minister.
他在60年代末、70年代初去了布莱克本，采访了很多人，在工人俱乐部等地闲逛。这些人并不关注威斯敏斯特的政治。你知道，非常非常普通的普通人。鲍威尔的名字被一次又一次地提起。他是这个国家最优秀的人。他应该成为首相。

112
00:57:29.000 --> 00:57:56.000
He speaks the mind of all the white, well, three quarters of the white people in this country. You know, you see this again and again. He wins polls. He won a BBC poll twice in the 1970s, man of the year. I mean, this is BBC listeners. And he is a wintry and ascetic lover of dead languages. But in a weird way, I think that kind of contributes to the appeal, right? That this guy has descended from an ivory tower on top of Mount Olympus to come down to speak for the masses.
他说出了这个国家所有白人，嗯，四分之三白人的心声。你知道，你一次又一次地看到这一点。他赢得了民意调查。他在20世纪70年代两次赢得BBC的年度人物民意调查。我的意思是，这是BBC的听众。他是一个冷峻而苦行的死语言爱好者。但以一种奇怪的方式，我认为这反而增加了他的吸引力，对吧？这个家伙从奥林匹斯山顶的象牙塔走下来，为大众说话。

113
00:57:56.000 --> 00:58:27.000
The flip side is he destroys his political career forever with this speech. So a lot of MPs shun him, actually not his mate Michael Foote in particular in the future leader of the Labour Party, who actually makes a point after the speech of going. and shaking his hand, you know, and being friendly to him, showing other MPs that he still is going to be Enoch's friend. Isn't that interesting? Anyway, two things that actually get lost to me in the talk about immigration. We mentioned at the beginning.
另一方面，他因为这次演讲永远毁掉了自己的政治生涯。所以很多议员避开他，实际上他未来的工党领袖迈克尔·富特并不是这样，他在演讲后特意去和他握手，你知道，对他很友好，向其他议员表明他仍然会是伊诺克的朋友。这难道不有趣吗？总之，在关于移民的讨论中，有两件事实际上被我忽略了。我们在开头提到了。

114
00:58:27.000 --> 00:59:06.000
In the next few years, Powell establishes what becomes the sort of economic gospel of Thatcherism, criticizing Ted Heath, for example, in the early 70s for spending too much and whatnot. And then when Britain joins Europe in 1973, Powell is the chief critic. He's the guy who basically invents Euroscepticism. So, you know, as his biographer, Simon Heffer says, if you judge a politician by his intellectual legacy, then he kind of stands alone in his generation because he basically bequeaths two things that are massively important in changing Britain. But he does it from the wilderness. Indeed, he ends up becoming an Ulster Unionist MP, not a Tory MP.
在接下来的几年里，鲍威尔确立了后来成为撒切尔主义经济福音的东西，例如在70年代初批评泰德·希思支出过多等等。然后当英国在1973年加入欧洲时，鲍威尔是主要的批评者。他基本上是发明了欧洲怀疑论的人。所以，正如他的传记作者西蒙·赫弗所说，如果你通过政治家的知识遗产来评判他，那么他在他那一代人中是独一无二的，因为他基本上留下了两件对改变英国至关重要的东西。但他是在荒野中完成这一切的。事实上，他最终成为了一名阿尔斯特统一党议员，而不是保守党议员。

115
00:59:06.000 --> 01:00:19.000
And I suppose this idea of a politician being massively influential on British history, despite not holding high office or becoming prime minister or whatever, that is also what a lot of people say about Nigel Farage. And I suppose the contrast with Farage and Powell is precisely that Powell is a massive intellectual, as well as someone who is capable of serving as a lightning rod for opinions that otherwise are not being articulated. Yeah, I think that's true. I think that's true. Now, I think some people listening to this would say, look, it's very unfair to just do this all about immigration. You're distorting Powell's record because he has so much more to offer. I have to say I don't think it's unfair because the point about the rivers of blood speech is not that it's just one speech. Power returns to the issue again and again in the next few years. A good example, really good example, 1972, Idi Amin kicked out Ugandan Asians from Uganda. And Powell led to the campaign to stop them. them coming to Britain. And he said, many white people in Britain feel as if they are tied to a stake in the face of an advancing tide. You know, the advancing tide image, really. That's the one you go for.
我想，尽管没有担任高级职务或成为首相，但一位政治家对英国历史产生巨大影响的这种想法，也是很多人对奈杰尔·法拉奇的评价。我想法拉奇和鲍威尔之间的对比恰恰在于，鲍威尔是一位伟大的知识分子，同时也是一个能够充当避雷针的人，表达那些原本没有被表达出来的观点。是的，我认为这是真的。我认为这是真的。现在，我想听这个节目的一些人会说，看，把这一切都归咎于移民是非常不公平的。你在歪曲鲍威尔的记录，因为他还有更多的东西可以提供。我必须说，我不认为这不公平，因为关于“血河”演讲的重点不是它只是一个演讲。鲍威尔在接下来的几年里一次又一次地回到这个问题上。一个很好的例子，非常好的例子，1972年，伊迪·阿明将乌干达亚裔赶出了乌干达。鲍威尔领导了阻止他们来到英国的运动。他说，英国的许多白人感觉就像在不断上涨的潮水面前被绑在木桩上一样。你知道，不断上涨的潮水的形象，真的。这就是你选择的那个。

116
01:00:19.000 --> 01:00:59.000
Now, we're talking about 27,000 people, middle class people by and large, who became one of the most successful of all immigrant communities. So the Tory politician, Priti Patel, her parents were Ugandan Asians. And actually, some listeners, even listeners sympathetic to people, Powell, I would say, might well ask themselves, was he right to say that none of these people should have been allowed in? Because actually the Ugandan Asian community proved enormously successful, assimilated very smoothly. And why did he, you know, he talks about the white people frightened and powerless. But he doesn't really talk about the Ugandan Asians who've been kicked
现在，我们谈论的是27000人，大体上是中产阶级，他们成为了最成功的移民群体之一。所以保守党政治家普里蒂·帕特尔，她的父母就是乌干达亚裔。实际上，一些听众，甚至是同情鲍威尔的听众，我想说，很可能会问自己，他当时说这些人都不应该被允许进入是对的吗？因为实际上乌干达亚裔社区证明是非常成功的，非常顺利地融入了社会。为什么他，你知道，他谈到白人感到恐惧和无力。但他并没有真正谈论那些被赶出自己国家、无处可去、感到恐惧和无力的乌干达亚裔难民。

117
01:00:59.000 --> 01:01:34.000
out of their country, have nowhere to go of frightened and powerless refugees. I think that would be one of my main criticisms of Powell. That's sort of the lack of empathy, if you like. Anyway, he keeps beating this drum throughout the 1970s. I mean, he uses some pretty striking language. When he looks into the eyes of Asia, the Englishman comes face to face with those who would dispute with him the possession of his native land. Again, you could hardly choose a more emotive and incendiary image, right?
我认为这将是我对鲍威尔的主要批评之一。如果可以的话，那就是缺乏同理心。总之，他在整个70年代一直在敲打这个鼓。我的意思是，他使用了一些非常引人注目的语言。当他注视着亚洲的眼睛时，英国人与那些将与他争夺这片土地所有权的人面对面。同样，你几乎找不到比这更感性和煽动性的形象了，对吧？

118
01:01:34.000 --> 01:02:01.000
struggling for possession of land with these people? Again, I mean, he presumably he's saying this because he fears, well, rivers of blood, but the language he is using is making that more likely, isn't it? I mean, it's... Yeah, I mean, I would say so, actually. To give, put my cards on the table, I always slightly sigh and roll my eyes when people just say in a very sort of blanket, condemnatory way, oh, in a pal, terrible racist, end of story.
与这些人争夺土地所有权？再说一次，我的意思是，他大概说这话是因为他害怕，嗯，血河，但他使用的语言正在使这种情况更有可能发生，不是吗？我的意思是，它是……是的，我的意思是，我确实会这么说。为了表明我的立场，每当人们以一种非常笼统、谴责的方式说，哦，伊诺克·鲍威尔，可怕的种族主义者，故事结束了，我总是会稍微叹气并翻个白眼。

119
01:02:01.000 --> 01:02:30.000
Because I always think, no, it's never the end of the story. There's always more nuanced and there's always an interesting history behind this. But when you read those words, I think it's very, very hard to defend these choices. And actually, to go to this question of, is Enoch Powell a racist? The word racist is so loaded and so complicated. It depends what you mean, of course. Its meaning has evolved quite drastically over the past decades. It's become a far more capacious, hasn't it?
因为我总是认为，不，这绝不是故事的结局。总有更细微的差别，背后总有一段有趣的历史。但当你读到这些话时，我认为很难为这些选择辩护。实际上，回到这个问题，伊诺克·鲍威尔是种族主义者吗？“种族主义者”这个词太沉重、太复杂了。当然，这取决于你的意思。在过去的几十年里，它的含义已经发生了巨大的演变。它变得更加宽泛了，不是吗？

120
01:02:30.000 --> 01:02:50.000
Yeah, it's capacious. Does he hate all foreigners? Clearly, no, he was hardly. going to be teaching himself all these languages if he hates foreigners. Is he prejudiced against individual people based on their skin colour? There's actually no evidence of that. He seems to have treated his foreign-born constituents exactly the same as his native-born constituents.
是的，它很宽泛。他讨厌所有的外国人吗？显然不是，如果他讨厌外国人，他就不太可能自学所有这些语言。他是否基于肤色对个人有偏见？实际上没有证据表明这一点。他似乎对待外国出生的选民与对待本土出生的选民完全一样。

121
01:02:50.000 --> 01:03:12.000
So again, and you'll be able to tell that the focus of my research for this episode has very much been Desert Island Discs. But Sue Lawley puts that question to him, and he says, no, he'd feel just the same about a load of French people coming over. So maybe that's expressive of his views on the Norman conquest, I don't know. Does he believe in racial hierarchy? He says very clearly, no, I do not. I do not believe that one race is superior to another.
所以，你能够看出我这一集的研究重点非常集中在《荒岛唱片》上。但苏·劳利向他提出了这个问题，他说，不，如果有一群法国人过来，他也会有同样的感觉。所以也许这表达了他对诺曼征服的看法，我不知道。他相信种族等级制度吗？他非常清楚地说，不，我不相信。我不相信一个种族优于另一个种族。

122
01:03:12.000 --> 01:03:31.000
Does he believe there are such things as races? Yes, he absolutely does. Racial difference is an undeniable truth. Now, here's something I would absolutely disagree with him about. He says, I think it is impossible that black and Asian people can become English. He says that they have no investment in England and its history. They can't be English. They'll never be English.
他相信有种族这种东西吗？是的，他绝对相信。种族差异是一个不可否认的事实。现在，这是我绝对不同意他的一点。他说，我认为黑人和亚裔不可能成为英国人。他说他们对英国及其历史没有投入。他们不可能是英国人。他们永远不会是英国人。

123
01:03:31.000 --> 01:03:54.000
their children will not be English. They are, you would often use the words, an alien element. Now, in other words, he would say that Rishi Sunag, or the current conservative leader, Kemi Badenock, cannot be English. Now, listeners, I think, can decide for themselves whether they agree with him or whether they think that's racist. It's not for me to tell them what to think. I mean, I don't agree with him.
他们的孩子也不会是英国人。他们是，你经常会用“外来元素”这个词。现在，换句话说，他会说里希·苏纳克或现任保守党领袖凯米·巴德诺克不可能是英国人。现在，听众们，我想，可以自己决定他们是否同意他的观点，或者他们是否认为那是种族主义。我不应该告诉他们该怎么想。我的意思是，我不同意他的观点。

124
01:03:54.000 --> 01:04:28.000
I think Kami Bidnok or Rishi Sunak, I think they are English. I mean, it's an interesting question. And there's another dimension to this that personally I find impossible to ignore. He always talked about the feelings of his white constituents, but almost never those of his black and Asian ones. And he never really seemed to consider the impact of his words. So there are lots of stories about incidents after his speech. Just one. There was a christening party in Wolverhampton, 10 days afterwards, so at the end of April, 1968, for a black family.
我认为凯米·巴德诺克或里希·苏纳克，我认为他们是英国人。我的意思是，这是一个有趣的问题。还有一个维度，我个人觉得无法忽视。他总是谈论他的白人选民的感受，但几乎从不谈论他的黑人和亚裔选民的感受。他似乎从未真正考虑过他言论的影响。所以有很多关于他演讲后发生的事件的故事。仅举一例。1968年4月底，也就是演讲10天后，在伍尔弗汉普顿有一个黑人家庭的洗礼派对。

125
01:04:28.000 --> 01:04:50.000
and the christening party was attacked by white youths with knives who were chanting Enoch Powell's name. The grandfather was a guy called Wade Crooks and he had to be taken for treatment because he needed eight stitches after being slashed over his eye. And he said he'd lived in Wolverhampton since 1955, nothing like this has ever happened before. And there are lots of incidents like this.
洗礼派对遭到了挥舞着刀子的白人青年的袭击，他们高呼伊诺克·鲍威尔的名字。祖父是一个叫韦德·克鲁克斯的人，他不得不接受治疗，因为他在眼睛被割伤后需要缝八针。他说他自1955年以来一直住在伍尔弗汉普顿，以前从未发生过这样的事情。像这样的事件有很多。

126
01:04:50.000 --> 01:05:22.000
Now on television, David Frost, big interview of the day, asked Enoch Powell to condemn these incidents. And Powell said, no. I'm not going to start condemning the behavior of people who are condemned by their own actions. It is not for a politician to be a preacher. I would say, is it so difficult really to condemn these actions? You know, clearly it's incumbent on you at this point if you have some responsibility to say, I would, if people are chanting your name, Tom, I mean, come on, would you not feel, of course you'd feel terrible about that. But it's interesting also that
现在在电视上，当天的重头采访大卫·弗罗斯特要求伊诺克·鲍威尔谴责这些事件。鲍威尔说，不。我不会开始谴责那些被自己的行为所谴责的人的行为。政治家不应该是传教士。我会说，谴责这些行为真的那么难吗？你知道，如果你有某种责任，此时此刻显然有义务说，如果人们高呼你的名字，汤姆，我的意思是，拜托，难道你不会感到，当然你会对此感到糟糕。但有趣的是，

127
01:05:22.000 --> 01:06:07.000
essentially the implication of that is that a politician should not have a moral perspective and promoted. I mean, that's basically what being a preacher is. And he is, I mean, he does all the time. I mean, his speech about Kenya was an example of preaching. The reason he doesn't want to do it, it's so, it's obvious and it's so familiar. He doesn't want to condemn it because he doesn't want to give an inch to his critics. I think it's pride and stubbornness. And that, I would argue, leads him to take positions that would call. me concern if I was taking them, right? If I knew that people on the far right, gangs of skinheads were chanting my name as they attacked immigrants, if I knew, to quote a Times
